Extend the vm_page_wire_count checkpoint chain past PE_lockdown_iokit (judyp bring-up).

The R-CK patch (zzzzzzzz-native-iokit-rootnub.patch -- one 'z' less) instrumented N=0..26 and
left the chain blind exactly at the point where the panel's stage history ends
(PE_lockdown_iokit) and where the sampled PC lives (_memmove+0xc4, sampled for 8 s).  This
patch adds checkpoints 27..29 WITHOUT touching any 0..26 site, so a single photograph of the
new kernel still answers the R-CK reading first (N=(wired-56772)/4, N<=26 means the old
reading applies verbatim) and only then the new segment:

 27 PE_lockdown_iokit returned   28 kperf_init_early returned   29 about to enter bsd_init

The former 30..33 sites in bsd_init.c are dropped: any insertion there lands inside the trailing
context of a shared patch's hunk (zzz-boot-progress.patch at `#endif` / `throttle_init`), and the
CI build replays every patch backwards on the already-patched tree, so the reverse check would fail.

Caveat recorded in EXPERIMENTS.md: past IOKit matching, vm_page_wire_count also grows
naturally (vfsinit, buffer cache, mountroot), so N>=29 readings can be polluted by natural
wiring.  A value that is 56772+4*N with N in 27..29 and no other panel change is still a
strong ordering signal; a wildly larger value means execution ran past IOKit and the stall
is inside or after bsd_init.
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -880,9 +880,15 @@
 	kernel_bootstrap_log("kernel_do_post - done");
 #endif /* CONFIG_XNUPOST */
 
+/* UNI_CK: see zzzzzzz-native-iokit-rootnub.patch -- every site bumps vm_page_wire_count
+ * by 4; sites 27..29 continue the table in pexpert/arm/pe_init.c past PE_lockdown_iokit. */
+extern unsigned int vm_page_wire_count;
+
 #ifdef  IOKIT
 	kernel_bootstrap_log("PE_lockdown_iokit");
 	PE_lockdown_iokit();
+	/* UNI_CK #27: PE_lockdown_iokit returned. */
+	vm_page_wire_count += 4;
 #endif
 	/*
 	 * max_cpus must be nailed down by the time PE_lockdown_iokit() finishes,
@@ -893,12 +899,16 @@
 
 #if KPERF
 	kperf_init_early();
+	/* UNI_CK #28: kperf_init_early returned. */
+	vm_page_wire_count += 4;
 #endif
 
 	/*
 	 *	Start the user bootstrap.
 	 */
 #ifdef  MACH_BSD
+	/* UNI_CK #29: about to enter bsd_init. */
+	vm_page_wire_count += 4;
 	bsd_init();
 #endif
 
